// MISCONFIGURED - no persistent volume defined for the database
services:
  db:
    image: mongo:7
    # NO volumes configuration at all

// SCENARIO: the team writes real, genuine user data to this
// database, then later runs 'docker compose down' followed by
// 'docker compose up' (a genuinely routine operation - perhaps
// upgrading the MongoDB image version, or simply restarting
// services after a host machine reboot)

// RESULT: the new 'db' container starts completely FRESH, from the
// base mongo:7 image - ALL previously-written user data is
// GENUINELY, PERMANENTLY GONE, since it only ever existed inside the
// PREVIOUS container's own, now-deleted, ephemeral filesystem

// CORRECTLY CONFIGURED - with a persistent volume
services:
  db:
    image: mongo:7
    volumes:
      - mongo-data:/data/db  # MongoDB's actual data directory,
                                # explicitly mounted to PERSISTENT storage

volumes:
  mongo-data:

// SAME SCENARIO: 'docker compose down' followed by 'docker compose
// up' - the NEW 'db' container starts fresh, but Docker
// automatically, correctly RE-ATTACHES the SAME persistent
// 'mongo-data' volume to this new container's /data/db path -
// EVERY single piece of previously-written user data is genuinely,
// completely intact and immediately available again

// A specific pod (one of the 3 replicas) genuinely crashes or
// becomes unresponsive - its /health endpoint (Module 25 lesson 4's
// exact endpoint) starts failing

// STEP 1 - Kubernetes's livenessProbe detects this failure within
// periodSeconds (10 seconds, in this lesson's example) - directly
// replacing Module 27 lesson 4's hand-written checkInstanceHealth
// polling loop

// STEP 2 - the Service (the automatic load balancer) immediately
// stops routing any NEW traffic to this specific, now-unhealthy pod
// - directly replacing Module 27 lesson 4's healthyInstances filter

// STEP 3 - Kubernetes AUTOMATICALLY terminates the failed pod and
// starts a genuinely NEW one from the SAME image, to maintain the
// Deployment's own, declared 'replicas: 3' desired state - directly
// replacing the manual restart/recovery logic a developer would
// otherwise need to build and operate themselves

// STEP 4 - once the NEW pod's own /health endpoint reports healthy,
// the Service automatically begins routing traffic to it again -
// directly replacing Module 25 lesson 5's hand-written, health-
// gated 'add back to load balancer' deployment-script step

// THE GENUINE RESULT: a developer who has ALREADY learned and built
// every one of these four steps manually, in Modules 25 and 27,
// recognizes Kubernetes as providing this EXACT same logic, now as
// a genuine, automatic, continuously-running platform capability

// A genuine bug: a recent code change accidentally hardcoded a
// PRODUCTION-only API key directly into the application, meaning it
// will genuinely FAIL when actually exercised in the staging
// environment, which has its own, genuinely separate, different key

// STAGE: test - PASSES. The unit test suite mocks the external API
// entirely, so this hardcoded-key bug is never actually exercised here.

// STAGE: build - PASSES. The Docker image builds successfully; a
// hardcoded API key doesn't cause any genuine BUILD failure.

// STAGE: deploy-staging - PASSES. The container starts successfully,
// and its /health endpoint (which doesn't itself call the external
// API) reports healthy - kubectl rollout status genuinely succeeds.

// STAGE: smoke-test-staging - FAILS. The smoke test suite makes a
// REAL request to a feature that genuinely depends on the external
// API, and the hardcoded, WRONG (production) key genuinely fails
// authentication against staging's own, different API endpoint

// RESULT: deploy-production's 'needs: smoke-test-staging' dependency
// is NOT satisfied - this broken build is CORRECTLY, COMPLETELY
// blocked from ever reaching production, specifically because this
// pipeline's smoke-test stage actually EXERCISED the real,
// deployed application's genuine behavior, rather than stopping at
// merely confirming the container started and reported healthy

// BASELINE: 3 replicas running (minReplicas), each averaging 30% CPU
// utilization - comfortably below the 70% target threshold

// A genuine, real traffic spike begins (e.g., a marketing campaign
// launches) - average CPU utilization across the 3 existing pods
// climbs to 85%, genuinely exceeding the configured 70% target

// THE HPA's CONTINUOUS MONITORING detects this real, measured
// excess and calculates a new, target replica count using a
// straightforward, evidence-based formula:
// desiredReplicas = currentReplicas * (currentUtilization / targetUtilization)
// desiredReplicas = 3 * (85 / 70) = ~3.6, rounded up to 4

// The HPA automatically increases replicas to 4 - lesson 3's
// Deployment controller (already covered) then automatically
// creates this 4th pod, with its OWN livenessProbe-gated startup
// process before it begins receiving genuine traffic

// IF traffic continues climbing, this exact calculation repeats
// continuously, automatically scaling further, up to maxReplicas: 20

// ONCE the traffic spike genuinely subsides and average CPU
// utilization drops back below 70% for a sustained, configured
// period, the HPA automatically SCALES BACK DOWN - but NEVER below
// minReplicas: 3, preserving Module 27 lesson 3's redundancy floor
// even during the genuinely lowest-traffic periods

