// COST ATTRIBUTION:

// 1. Production baseline: 4 replicas x $40/month = $160/month

// 2. Staging (lesson 4): identical 4 replicas x $40/month =
// ADDITIONAL $160/month, purely for staging's own safety value

// 3. Auto-scaling variable cost (lesson 5): average 7 replicas
// during business hours means 3 ADDITIONAL replicas beyond the
// 4-replica baseline, roughly 3 x $40/month = ADDITIONAL
// ~$120/month (a simplified estimate, since this varies with actual usage)

// 4. Distributed cache (Module 29 lesson 4): 2 nodes x $25/month =
// ADDITIONAL $50/month

// TOTAL ESTIMATED MONTHLY COST: ~$490/month

// EVALUATION:
// - Staging's $160/month is justified IF the team genuinely,
//   consistently relies on it as a mandatory gate (Module 25 lesson
//   3's argument) - if staging is rarely actually used, this cost
//   deserves re-examination.
// - The distributed cache's $50/month was ORIGINALLY justified by a
//   specific, measured latency improvement (Module 29 lesson 4) -
//   this audit should confirm that improvement remains genuinely
//   measurable and worth this ongoing cost, not simply assume the
//   original justification still holds indefinitely.
// - The auto-scaling variable cost reflects genuine, real demand and
//   is inherently harder to "reduce" without genuinely reducing
//   either traffic or per-request resource consumption.
